so where's this getting the political  parties joining me now from westminster  are philip blonde uh who is a former  advisor to david cameron and the  director of the think tank res publica  also in central london is kevin craig  the managing director of political  lobbying and media relations uh the  communications company thank you both uh  for joining us uh mr craig if i could uh  start with you  do you think that this is a winner for  ed miliband in terms of getting the  votes he needs to win a general election  it's a winner in helping ed miliband set  the agenda he's uh for a long time been  engaged on a very clear core vote  strategy and he wants us not to be  talking about immigration which 42  percent of people say is their number  one concern but he wants him to be  talking about equality and are you  getting a fair slice of this economy in  this society so for him very effective  today because he managed he called out  lord fink who is now not going to  launch legal action so it's a short-term  win and very effective but this will not  translate yet into changing votes in the  number of key seats where the election  victory lies why not  well because that happens over a  sustained period of time it happens in  the hundreds of thousands of  conversations that are being undertaken  by canvases week in week out this issue  at the moment is a big deal around  westminster people are thinking that ed  miliband's been bold and courageous he  has to be careful the pushback against  him has already started on a for for  example on guido fawkes tonight but  these things don't meet real people for  weeks and it's good for ed miliband it  makes him look decisive and effective  but it's far too early to say it's going  to translate into changing minds in  thorac or other key constituencies  um mr blonde if labour can persuade the  majority that people better off than  them  are effectively rich and also bad people  that could be a really crucial election  winner couldn't it for labor  well it certainly could and at the  moment according to the latest poll um  on this issue 49 of people think that ed  miliband was standing up for  ordinary people in this affair with mr  fink against uh less than half of that  who thought uh he'd be bad for the  economy so i mean i agree i think he's  winning on this but labour's winning on  this issue in all sorts of ways labour  is thought to be better but standing up  for you and your family better for  keeping down the price of everyday items  better for affordable housing where it's  losing ground is as  is that it remains within its core vote  it doesn't speak to the aspirational  classes it doesn't speak to the millions  of people who want to own their business  and what i find inexplicable in this  strategy is i understand why the tories  are following a core vote strategy  because they're threatened by ukip on  their right flank but why oh why is  labour following a corvette strategy um  it's got to move into the middle ground  if it wants to win and it's as if  they've they're in front of an open goal  but with no goalkeeper and they keep  blasting it over uh the the top of the  post  it's it's inexplicable really let's put  that to uh kevin craig there why is mr  miliband going down this route if it is  polarizing and appeals to the core  rather than to the centrist floating  voter  listen i respect phillip very much but  labor and ed miliband to his credit has  had a strategy for a long time which is  it's not 1997. he made a long time ago a  calculated decision that this country  has moved slightly to the left from  where it was before he's also made a  calculation that labor if it gets out  the core vote and if it resonates  in areas of where where that core vote  is key it will succeed and let's not  forget labor is fighting for the left in  scotland where that all those seats have  come into play and ed miliband and the  people around him they know that he's  not strong on some personal presentation  issues they know he's not strong in in  in this sort of more blair-right agenda  but their calculation which they're very  open about is that people want to feel  society is better  and working for the many not the few and  so one can disagree with it um and as  philip says but that's their strategy  and that they're not going to be  deflected it's time to in their view  batten down the hatches and yes i can  tell you there's a lot of concern  amongst some labour business people that  i talk to about the rhetoric but then  the answer back to them from labour hq  is guys there's an election to be won  here some of this rhetoric is stronger  than maybe we'll implement but we need  to get the core vote out people need to  feel there's a point in voting working  class people people at the lower end  they've just given up on politics thank  you for that kevin let's put that to  philip blom there's your answer  well i think it's a very good answer and  i think i think kevin's right but i  think the strategy is flawed um because  you can do both you can keep your call  and move to the center almost all  successful political leaders who command  the sort of majority that you'd need to  command nowadays uh have done that in  the past and it would be quite easy  think of the distinction miliband made  between predator and producer that was  actually very effective but it was never  fully deployed for all of his attacks on  the wrong source big business he hasn't  really talked about the right sort of  business he hasn't talked about what a  successful small business would need he  hasn't really polarized his own  opposition he could get an enormous  amount of support amongst the small  business community if they created the  sort of offer that would appeal to them  because it is true they also feel shut  out so what i'm saying is actually the  corvo strategy is wrong because the core  is actually much larger there is a large  majority of people who feel they are not  winning from the current economic  settlement but labour isn't speaking to  them okay they're going to have to make  sure they don't need to do much more  thank you both very much uh kevin and  philip there  only 85 days until we find out who's  right

